  "mechanismSummary": "W. S. Johnson's progesterone synthesis (1971) builds three rings in one step from an open chain, as nature makes lanosterol from squalene oxide. The cascade needs a cation to start it, the *initiator*. Johnson used a 1,3-dimethylcyclopent-2-en-1-ol carrying the chain at the middle carbon of the alkene: trifluoroacetic acid protonates the OH and water leaves. The cation is tertiary and allylic, shared equally by the two methyl-bearing carbons, so it forms quickly at −70 °C without any other carbon of the chain being protonated. The alkenes of the chain are the nucleophiles of the next stage.",
